Z||000006||Monkeyman||10-12-2000||09:33 PM||||I haven't solved it in years.  I have a thin paperback book that explains all of the moves required to solve it.  It now seems that the solution is on the web, too.  I think half the fun is being able to move/rotate just one (or a combination) cube.  Once I re-master the Rubiks Cube, my next challenge will be the Professors Cube.  Same principle as the Rubiks Cube, but more faces.  The Rubiks Cube is 3x3x3, where as the Professors Cube is 5x5x5!  That should be a challenge.  The worlds record for the Rubiks Cube is under a minute, 10 times in a row, using differently configured cubes.  I think I read that the record for the Professors Cube (using the same rules) is somewhere in the neighborhood of 10 minutes.<P>BTW--I did a search for the Pyraminx.  I found the site of the creator.  He sells them for $16US (plus an enormous amount of postage I'm sure since he appears to be in Germany).  He also sells lots of other puzzles that are similar.  '80s, here I come!||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-12-2000||10:54 PM||||I'm still in the 80's... been playing old arcade games. Nothing like the original arcade versions of a game. Gyrus and Rampage are my favorites. Marble Madness and Quantum again now that I have the trackball.||||reg||1
